1. What am I expected to already know?
You should know the difference between Usenet, email, the WWW, and other 
parts of the Internet - or at the very least, you should recognize that 
the Internet is more than just the World Wide Web. (You're reading this 
group; we can only hope that you understand that there *is* a 
difference.) We expect you to know what a search engine is and how to 
use at least one. You are expected to know how to turn on your computer. 
You are expected to know how to be polite and courteous, and have good 
internalized reasons for doing so other than the fact that you want 
something from us. You are expected to be able to figure most things out 
on your own.

What's most important isn't anything that you might already know, but 
rather a willingness to learn more.


2. How do I hack?
That's a harder question than you might think. Someone once said: you 
don't learn to hack, you hack to learn. Hacking isn't an activity - it's 
a state of mind, a means to an end, and not an end unto itself. Hacking 
is *not* about stroking your ego, doing things that make headlines, or 
strutting and posturing.


3. Can you help me hack?
Can we? Well, we *are* able to do many things that fall under the 
general label "hacking".

Will we? That depends on what you mean by "hack". Generally speaking, 
the amount of help we give is determined by the specifics of the problem 
and not by your need. This is Alice's Restaurant, not a help desk.


3a. What *will* you help me with?
There are no guarantees that anyone will help you with anything, but 
here's what people are likely to help you with:
 - solve interesting problems
 - figure things out
 - learn, if you are able

Our typical teaching methods are Zen methods - instead of giving 
answers, we respond to questions with more questions. If those methods 
don't work, we might break out the clue bat, followed closely by the 
flamethrower. (A good saying to live by: "Seek not to know all the 
answers, but rather to understand the questions.")


3b. What *won't* you help me with?
Just as there are no guarantees that we *will* help you with something, 
there are no guarantees we *won't* help you either. Here is a reasonable 
list that you can expect us to *not* help with:
 - mentoring
 - homework
 - walk-throughs
 - crack a particular program (someone *might* give general advice)
 - how to break in somewhere
 - *anything* specifically illegal. (Even asking for such help is 
   considered ignorant - we may be *hackers*, that does not 
   automatically make us *criminals*. Illegal acts by others *DO NOT* 
   justify illegal acts by you or by us.)
 - revenge (we might offer advice) (See also #13a, "Revenge".)


4. The hacker ethic?
The definition of "the hacker ethic" that you get can depend on whom you 
ask, as there are (at least) 2 common definitions. Here's a brief 
discussion in the Jargon File:
    http://www.catb.org/~esr/jargon/html/H/hacker-ethic.html

Personally, I (Auric__) don't regard information sharing as particularly 
ethical or unethical - open-source software isn't a matter of ethics. To 
me, the hacker ethic is pretty close to definition 2 in the Jargon File: 
do as thou wilt, but do no damage and do no harm.


5. What kinds of activities should I avoid? Why?
 - Don't use anything that acts even remotely like a virus (this 
   includes programs that use Trojan-like activity to sneak into a 
   system). Using a virus for any reason opens you up to some serious 
   prosecution (and the courts are getting less lenient every day).

 - Don't damage any systems you manage to get access to - again, 
   prosecution, plus it's not very nice.

 - If you download something that isn't yours, do NOT post it anywhere! 
   I can't stress this enough - posting stolen files anywhere is the 
   quickest route to sharing a small jail cell with a big man named 
   Bubba (can you say "be my bitch"?) that I can think of (short of 
   writing a virus), especially if they happen to contain info that 
   shouldn't become public knowledge, such as credit card information or 
   matters of national security.

 - Don't use anything you happen to find for financial gain - not only 
   is that unethical, it's also usually illegal and often easily detected.

 - If you download something, don't post it or spread it around looking 
   for some kind of fucked-up prestige. If you want people to know that 
   you've managed to break into the CIA, or some large bank, or 
   anywhere, invite them over and let them watch over your shoulder.

 - Don't make threats. If you tell someone that you intend to do bad 
   things to them in any way, chances are you have already committed a 
   crime. If you're going to do something illegal, just do it and be a 
   criminal. Empty threats are for cowards and losers.


6. Social engineering?
Merriam-Webster defines social engineering as "management of human 
beings in accordance with their place and function in society : applied 
social science".

As far as hacking is concerned, social engineering is the art of getting 
information out of people without those people realizing that they've 
given it to the wrong people. For a good definition plus examples, go 
here:
    http://www.catb.org/~esr/jargon/html/S/social-engineering.html

SecurityFocus has an in-depth discussion of social engineering, 
including examples and strategies (both for and against) here:
    http://www.securityfocus.com/infocus/1527


7. What should I know to be able to hack?
Since the most common usage of "hacking" refers to computers, you should 
first get familiar with yours - learn the basics of the operating 
system, find out what programs are installed and what they're used for, 
etc.


8. What should I read?
Know your goal for what you would like to hack - for example, programs, 
operating systems, hardware, networks, people, etc. Then read everything 
you can get that's related to your desired subject(s). And feel free to 
ask here for the appropriate vocabulary if you're unsure and need to use 
a search engine.


9. What tools should I use?
The single most important tool in your kit is your brain. Fail to use it 
effectively and, well, you might as well just sell your computer and get 
a WebTV console instead.

If by "tools" you mean programs designed to do not-very-nice things to 
other people's computers, remember that, in general, hackers view such 
programs as crutches, or an admittance of inability. There is even a 
term for people who rely on such programs: "script kiddie". (In case you 
can't tell, it's not a compliment.)


9a. What operating system should I use?
Free is always good. Especially if it will install correctly on your 
machine and is supported with regular updates.

Not supporting a major world-wide monopoly that likes to dictate how you 
will go where they think you want to go today seems good, too.

Finally, any operating system that runs on a so-called "thin client" 
(a.k.a. diskless workstation), or requires file storage and applications 
to be installed on another remotely-located machine that you do not have 
immediate physical control over seems fundamentally bad.

Bearing all that in mind, the OS(s) you should use should be which ever 
one(s) you want to hack. If you are uncertain, just remember this: In 
general, graphical user interfaces are designed to make things simpler, 
but also make it harder to understand the basic workings of an operating 
system.


9b. What programming languages should I learn?
Whatever language(s) you have an immediate need for. Each has specific 
benefits and pitfalls, but knowledge of those will come with time.

Programming is about structured or ordered and rational thought. Some 
languages teach this better than others. (On the other hand: "There are 
no such thing as bad languages, only bad programmers.")

A general idea of structure and syntax is always good to know, as these 
have more universal application than any specific languages. An idea of 
what written commands actually do is another important step. After this, 
look for the "not"-type command that will invert the meaning of 
commands. Beginning and ending commands for loops and variable 
declarations are other good, small steps.

Languages can be either interpreted (scripts that can be executed after 
being written), or compiled (scripts that must be converted into an 
executable form to do anything). The most important difference between 
these two is the ability to read an interpreted script vs. the need to 
decompile the compiled program into assembly language to really 
understand it. You must decide whether you want the relative ease of an 
interpreted language or the speed of a compiled language

Look at your interests in what kind of programming you wish to do. 
Decide if you want the ease of an interpreted language or the speed of a 
compiled one. Then gorge on the buffet platter of languages.


9c. LAN tools?
Three basic tools included with most OS's are ping, netstat, and 
traceroute (tracert under Windows). To find out what each is used for, 
type the command followed by -? on the command line.

Other tools for use only on a private LAN (please do not ask us 
questions about these tools, they should come with help files):

 - port scanners: There are many out there.
   - NMAP has a stealth mode. This is a good tool to find out what
     unexpected security holes (open ports) different pieces of
     software may be causing.
   - IANA (Internet Assigned Numbers Authority, the people in charge of
     assigning IP addresses) keeps a list of assigned ports and their
     common uses here:
        http://www.iana.org/assignments/port-numbers
   - COTSE (The Church of the Swimming Elephant) has a tool to search
     for ports by name:
        http://www.cotse.com/cgi-bin/port.cgi

 - protocol analyzers/sniffers:
   - TCPView is small, and it just monitors TCP/UDP connections to and
     from your PC. Also helpful for finding unexpected connections to
     and from your machine that different programs may make. A trojan
     or a hidden server will show up here, even if it doesn't show up
     anywhere else.
   - Ethereal is big, requires a plugin, and will monitor all protocols
     & packets to and from your PC.


10. What's a shell account?
A shell account is a way to remotely logon to another computer, usually 
over port 21.

Basically, it's a way to tunnel to a proxy, or a remote PC, and use it 
as the actual computer, while your local PC is just a dumb terminal 
(a.k.a. "thin client" or "diskless workstation"; for those of you who 
want to be able to use your real-world "thin clients" for more than 
you're supposed to, this would be how).


10a. Why would I use a shell account?
Some people use them to try out a different OS. Others use them for 
faster downloads. Still others use them for even greater privacy than a 
conventional proxy can provide. Since you have control over the remote 
PC, you can erase any record you were ever there. This ranks near 
mail2news and nym-alias-net for anonymity.

In addition to the many things they can do that you just have to 
experience to believe, one can surf anonymously and download much faster 
than with even a cable connection, since one can remotely logon to a 
computer attached to an Internet backbone. Of course, this means that 
you must be able to function in whatever operating system is installed 
on that remote computer.


11. Can I hack from WebTV/AOL/etc.?
WebTV: Not really - at least, not what most non-hackers think hacking 
is. In fact, the WebTV console was designed to *prevent* hacking. (You 
could possibly hack the console itself, but that would probably void 
your warranty and also would likely go against their terms of service.)
AOL: AOL provides you with a connection to the Internet, so if your idea 
of hacking is "accessing other computers for questionable purposes" then 
yes, it is possible to hack over AOL.
Etc.: It largely depends on what your specific "etc." is, but chances 
are, yes, you can.

There *are* certain things you can do from pretty much any Internet 
connection to annoy other people, but why bother? At best, you just make 
people angry at you; at worst, you get your Internet account canceled, 
or worse. (Harassing someone over the Internet is still harassment - you 
can still go to jail even if it's not "real life".)


12. How can I hack a Hotmail/AOL/etc. account?
The only truly effective general-purpose method is to use social 
engineering (see #6, "Social engineering?") on the owner of the account. 
The large ISPs and email services (and indeed, most of the not-so-large 
ones) are by and large immune to hacking; they are well familiar with 
the various tricks and methods, and tend to install security patches as 
soon as they're available.


12a. But it's *my* account; I forgot my password!
If it's your ISP, call their customer support (or tech support) number 
and explain the situation. They deal with this sort of thing on a "more 
than once daily" basis and usually have methods for handling it.

If it's a free email service - such as Hotmail or Yahoo - then the first 
thing to do is find the "forgot password" link on the service's front 
page. Most, if not all, email services have a method for you to retrieve 
your password, usually by providing private information, or answering a 
previously-selected question. If you can't get your password that way, 
then by far the easiest thing to do is open a new account and write to 
all of your contacts letting them know what happened. No, you can't 
"hack in" to retrieve your contact list - consider it lost.

If it's a paid email service, first treat the problem as you would a 
free email service - look for a "forgot password" link, etc. If that 
fails, then treat it as you would your ISP - call their customer support 
number.


12b. What about exploiting Hotmail's internal tech support address?
There is no such thing. The actual exploit is conning you into sending 
your email address and password to someone else. This is a perfect 
example of social engineering.


13. Any more advice?
 - Before you post here (or anywhere, Usenet or otherwise), LEARN BASIC 
   SPELLING AND GRAMMAR. There are already far too many idiots already 
   who POAST THNIGS LEIK THES!!!!!!11!!!one!!! (Argh, my brain just 
   exploded.) Also don't use excessive abbreviations, and *never* 
   abbreviate an entire word down to a single letter, or to a number if 
   that number isn't relevant (examples: "Ppl, plz hlp me w/this!" "R u 
   goin 2 help?" "OU812"). And remember, posting in ALL CAPITAL LETTERS 
   is considered *yelling*. Use all-caps sparely if at all.

Perhaps you have read somewhere that proper spelling and grammar aren't 
as important on the Internet as they are in, say, your term paper, or a 
newspaper article. Nothing could be farther from the truth. Your writing 
style reflects who you are and how you think; the better you write, the 
higher other people's opinions of you (generally). Many newsreaders have 
this neat function called spell check - learn how to use it. (If you're 
using a Microsoft product, just hit F7.) If your newsreader doesn't have 
a spell-check function, most word processors do. If you have neither, go 
to this website:
    http://www.spellcheck.net/

 - Forget everything that you've ever heard, read, or thought about 
   hacking - most of the popular image of hackers is crap. (I don't know 
   about you, but I'm not the classic nerd who wears horn-rimmed glasses 
   and a pocket protector, and never goes outside. This is *not* 
   "Revenge of the Nerds".) Forget what the press has to say about 
   hackers - 99% of what they think about ANYTHING is pure bullshit, and 
   their collective concept of hackers is damn close to 100% wrong. Also 
   forget about movies like "Swordfish", "Hackers", and "The Net" - 
   those movies do NOT show real hacking (but they do seem to get the 
   concept of "criminal behavior" down pat). If you want to see a movie 
   that shows something resembling a real hacker, see if you can find a 
   copy of "Wargames" - David (Matthew Broderick) is a good example of a 
   hacker (he even uses social engineering; when and where is left as an 
   exercise for the student).

 - Assuming that you're still thinking of the stereotypical concept of 
   hacking - that is, breaking into other computers across some sort of 
   telecommunications network - your best choice of programs is probably 
   telnet (or possibly rlogin, in *nix). Learn the basic commands and 
   get familiar with a *nix-like environment. Also learn what ports are 
   and which ports are used for which protocols. It wouldn't hurt to get 
   familiar with the OSI model, but that's not essential. Just remember, 
   it's not hacking if you're breaking in just for the sake of doing 
   damage.


13a. Revenge
If this is a friend or relative, talk to him/her. You can't have a 
relationship without the occasional disagreement. It's an opportunity to 
grow as an individual and improve the quality of said relationship. If 
you feel that you must strike out anonymously and spitefully, you need 
to re-evaluate your motives and feelings; therapy might be helpful.

If it is *not* someone close to you, you are far better off removing 
that person from your life (metaphorically, not physically) than trying 
to do them harm in any way.

Look at wars in history and you will see that fighting does not 
determine who is right - only who is left if weapons are used, and 
retaliation is no way to live. (Take this from someone who has had way 
too many close calls with the final curtain call.)


13b. "Help needed!"
Your subject line should relate to the content in a more specific 
manner. Rather than "help needed" or "I need help", try something like 
"please help me with _____" (fill in the blank).


13c. "Please respond by email"
The purpose for having a newsgroup is not so individuals could receive 
personal help by individuals. Newsgroups are here for having discussions 
that everyone can contribute to and benefit from.

If someone responds to some question that you asked via email, how do 
you know if that information is accurate? How do you know that the 
information will work for you? What if there's a newer, more efficient, 
or more accurate method that the person emailing you hasn't heard of 
yet? What if someone else comes along and happens to have the same or a 
similar problem as you?

Besides those concerns, responding via email poses 2 very real hazards: 
one of compromising personal privacy, and one of abuse.

All of these problems could be eliminated by using the newsgroup instead 
of private email. Others can see the posts and add information or ideas 
that they have (or correct information that others have posted), thus 
creating a greater chance of answering your question or solving your 
problem, and the answer would be available in archives, for later 
referral or rereading.


13d. "Hi, i No speak englisch verree gut but questin i hav"
If you are unable to speak English, you should post to a group in your 
native language to avoid unnecessary confusion (necessary confusion is a 
different matter). If you must post to an English group (specfically 
abhb, but really any group where the majority of the posting is in 
any one language) post in both your native language and the translated 
language. There are numerous web sites that can translate for free - and 
often better than non-native speakers can. I (Auric__) use Google and 
Altavista's Babelfish, but there are many others. (This FIA is available 
on the WWW in as many languages as I can get it translated into. Look at 
the beginning of this document for links.)


13e. "Fuck you, you fucking fuckers!"
Ah, yes, swearing - the crutch for the intellectually deviant. Posts of 
this nature don't usually have the intended effect, and sometimes turn 
into some really interesting discussion - usually how to get rid of 
such people. If you think that we are "evil people", then either go away 
and leave us to our own devices, or post an intelligent and well-thought-
out article stating your problem(s) with us and why it's a problem - if 
you need help, try these:
    http://www.dictionary.com
    http://www.notam02.no/~hcholm/altlang/

If you think we're being intentionally unhelpful, guess what - you're 
probably right.

Oh yeah, one other thing... "Go fuck yourself."


13f. "Bu+ 1 ju$+ w4|\|+ 2 b /\ l33t hax0r!"
Too bad you're a moron. (If you can't read that, I can't blame you. This 
section is called, "But I just want to be a leet haxor!"  "Leet" means 
"elite", "haxor" means "hacker".) Using "leetspeak" like this doesn't 
tell us that you're a hacker, it tells us that you're an idiot.

Welcome to Usenet; how you write is how you are perceived; who you 
appear to be. If you do not speak clearly and present yourself in a 
professional manner, you shall not be treated as such. If you act as if 
you have something to hide or should be suspect, you shall be treated as 
such.

It's also worth mentioning that being called a hacker by your friends, 
boss, or clueless coworkers is worthless - it doesn't mean anything 
unless a *hacker* calls you a hacker (and even then the value is 
negligible). Of course - this should go without saying, but I'll say it 
anyway - being called a hacker by anyone in law enforcement is generally 
a Bad Thing.


13g. Cracks, warez, pr0n, passwords, etc.
The members of this group do not specifically condone or specialize in 
such things as cracks (bypassing portions of programs typically used to 
ensure that the authors get paid), warez (commercial software obtained 
illegitimately), pr0n (pornography), exchanging passwords for programs 
or web sites, etc.

While each person's feelings about these items will obviously vary to a 
large degree, we are not here to provide you with these items. Sure, it 
might be an intellectual challenge to figure out the algorithm that a 
particular program uses to determine valid passwords, but it's not what 
we do. If that's what you want, pop over to alt.2600.hackerz and read 
the document by Harlequin entitled "Read this for CRACKS, WARES & XXX 
PASSWORDS."


14. Hmm... maybe I don't want to hack after all...
Good. If you're saying this to yourself, then you're right, you *don't* 
want to hack. However, there are games where you *play* at hacking, of 
you're interested in the idea of hacking but not the associated risks. 
<advert> My (Auric__) personal favorite is Uplink, by Introversion:
    http://www.uplink.co.uk/
Uplink is not free (in any sense of the word), is not Open Source (you 
can buy a copy of the source, but can't turn around and sell or give 
away the game), and is not realistic by any stretch of the imagination, 
but it *is* fun and, well, addictive. There is a free Windows demo of 
the game which, unfortunately, only lasts about an hour or so (in my 
experience). </advert>

Another game that's supposed to be good (I [Auric__] haven't tried it) 
is called, simply, "Hacker". It's old, but it tends to get good reviews 
at "classic games" sites. It even has a sequel called, unimaginatively, 
"Hacker 2".


15. Okay, but all I wanted was a secure system/better privacy.
Please excuse us for rambling on; if you read all this, then you have an 
idea of where our subculture stands.

Remember that firewalls are a must (read #15d, "Firewalls?"). Spyware 
removers and regular system scans are also required. Spybot Search and 
Destroy as well as Adaware are a fairly comprehensive tandem (see #15g, 
"Spyware and Homepage Hijacking?"). A startup monitor - a program that 
monitors other programs' requests to be inserted into your computer's 
startup sequence - is also a good idea.

As for security software, they are often not as secure as they would 
like you to think, and are rarely needed for the jobs they perform.


15a. Where do I start?
Keep your system up to date. If you're running Windows, you should check 
http://www.windowsupdate.com (or run wupdmgr.exe) on a regular basis 
(preferably daily) and install any security updates immediately. If 
you're running a *nix system, keep the various components of your system 
up-to-date.

Regardless of operating system, you should read computer security 
websites on a regular basis - those will tell you about the latest 
security problems on your system and (hopefully) how to fix them.


15b. Password protection schemes?
Way too often systems are broken into through weak (or non-existent) 
password security. For example, Windows XP is left wide open by virtue 
of home users not knowing to give a password to the default 
administrator account and/or making themselves a secondary administrator 
account also without a password. This leaves the system vulnerable to 
virii as well as to the visiting snoop.

A good method that we (Banduur & Auric__, likely others) have found is 
to rename the default administrator (or admin, or root, or...) account 
and have no more than two administrator accounts on the system. Programs 
can be run (in Windows) as administrator by holding down the shift or 
control key while right clicking and choose "run as" from the context 
menu, or (in Linux, possibly other *nix flavors) via the sudo command.

Passwords don't have to be obscure nonsense for the sake of safety (but 
note that obscure, nonsensical passwords would probably block most 
dictionary attacks -- Auric__). Know the limits of your OS's password 
methodology: Does it permit special characters? Which ones? Is it case 
sensitive? Use a mixed case and numeric password of 6 to 8 characters 
and avoid dictionary words in any language as well as reverse dictionary 
and "leetspeak" variations. Also avoid using things like your name, the 
names of family members/pets/your car/etc., or numbers associated with 
you (address, birthdate, parking space, social security number, etc. - 
but if someone else knows your SSN, you really should have bigger 
worries than just computer security).

Special note: the word "password" is a *terrible* password. Many 
companies use "password" as their default password, making it one of the 
first things that any sensible cracker would check when trying to force 
entry. If your password to any system is "password", STOP READING AND 
CHANGE IT RIGHT NOW! This also applies if you have **NO** password!


15c. Routers?
A router is, essentially, an intelligent repeater - it receives a 
signal, figures out what to do with it, then either forwards the signal 
to the appropriate destination or discards it.

Use a hardware router with NAT (network address translation). A software 
router installed on a computer (such as Wingate) is only as secure as 
the operating system installed on the PC.


15d. Firewalls?
A firewall is similar to a router - it too receives a signal, figures 
out what to do with it, and either forwards the signal or discards it. 
The difference is, a router is like the post office - if the address is 
valid, the message gets through - while a firewall is like a security 
guard - you must show that you belong where you're going to get past 
him. (It's more complex than that, of course, but that's the short short 
version.) Many, perhaps most routers are able to function as firewalls; 
indeed, that's often one of their main selling points.

Use a hardware firewall - the same machine as your router, if it can be 
set up to act as such. Software firewalls make a good backup to a 
hardware firewall, and are better than nothing if you can't or won't get 
a hardware firewall. In addition to protecting you, a firewall can 
inform you of outgoing information that you weren't aware of and/or 
don't want going out (such as when a program "phones home").


15e. Proxies?
Personal or filtering proxies keep unwanted Internet clutter, like 
adverts, cookies, and nosy sysops out of your PC, and personal data in. 
Try Proxomitron, or Privoxy. Anonymity proxies go further in that they 
hide your Internet IP behind another computer. Caching proxies won't 
help your privacy, only the speed at which you can download *some* web 
pages.


15f. Antivirus?
Use antivirus software, and keep it up to date. Norton/Symantec is as 
good as McAfee (or whatever they're calling themselves this week) is as 
good as any other antivirus company, despite what their marketing 
departments will tell you. Buy whichever one is on sale when you're at 
the store, or get a free one off the Internet. (There aren't many, but 
they are there; just keep looking, or ask in abhb.) If you are running 
Windows, you can fire up Internet Explorer and go to this site for a 
free online virus scan:
    http://housecall.antivirus.com/

Note: if you want to test your antivirus program to make sure it's 
working, don't use a real virus! That would be similar to making sure a 
bulletproof vest works by shooting yourself - you might survive the 
process, but you also might regret it afterward. Follow this link, read 
that page, download that file - it's not a real virus, just a test:
    http://www.eicar.org/anti_virus_test_file.htm


15g. Spyware and Homepage Hijacking?
Spyware is software that tracks what you do at your computer - usually 
for marketing purposes, but they can be used for even less ethical 
purposes. Anti-spyware programs are designed to hunt down and eradicate 
these programs, along with other privacy no-no's. Adaware and Spybot 
Search and Destroy are probably the two that are most used.

Homepage hijacking is when you visit a web page - often porn but not 
always (you can sometimes tell by what your homepage gets set to) that 
installs a program (with or without your knowledge) that ensures that 
your homepage is set to their designated location, even if you change it 
in your preferences. (No, folks, they are *not* viruses, your antivirus 
software will *not* catch this.) Anti-spyware programs usually are able 
to remove these programs - the two mentioned above, Adaware and Spybot, 
certainly can.


15h. Hosts file?
Imagine a way to tell your computer to look at /dev/null for almost all 
servers hosting ads that any web page might call. No 3rd party server, 
no ad. Before there was DNS to tell a browser what IP a name was, every 
computer had a manually entered text file that listed IP's and host 
names associated with them. That text file still exists. It's called the 
hosts file, and by default, the hosts file is checked before online DNS 
(and if a host has an entry, DNS is skipped).

If page A wants an ad from server B to sell you widgets, page A puts a 
link to server B, calling that ad. If your hosts file says server B is 
non-existent, the browser will skip ahead to the next line of HTML (or, 
at worst, give you a "host not found" error where the ad should be), 
ignoring DNS, and you get no ad. Make your hosts file read-only, with 
lots of security against overwrites. I (reverend maxwell snort) use 
Gorilla Design Studios, but undoubtedly there are other sources.


16. Conclusion.
There are many forms of "hacking". It's not so much about "how to hack" 
as it is "who or what you hack". Just because you read a few books on 
the subject does not qualify you as an expert on that subject. When you 
consistently read on a subject and *PRACTICE DAILY*, then you might 
begin to understand.

If you choose to use any tools or advice from here, please remember to 
keep it legal and as a means to bettering the people that you use it on 
and for. As much as some of us like being locked away in seclusion and 
left to our own devices, I for one choose to avoid anything that would 
lead to sharing a bunk with "Bubba".


17. WTF? RU4 reel?
You see, we don't play at being hackers. It's our lifestyle; everything 
we do is focused by it. The people I know outside of this place have no 
real idea what I can do, if I choose. I don't show off, I don't make 
idle threats, I don't brag. I do answer questions very carefully. when 
asked if I know about something, I usually answer, "I don't know, but I 
can find out." I even go through the motions of looking up information. 
I use a whole lot of social engineering.

I learned the phrase "you are what you think other people think you 
are", and I use it to get people to leave me to my own devices. I do not 
use fear or intimidation. I use the techniques taught by Dale Carnegie 
in the book "How To Win Friends and Influence People". I use the gentle 
art of letting other people have my way. Am I for real? I don't know, 
but I can find out.


